   "summary": "Published for upper secondary school students and adult education, Volume I defines democracy as a spiritual commitment to the inherent dignity, worth, and equality of every individual rather than merely a political structure. It contrasts democratic governance based on popular sovereignty and majority rule with autocracy, totalitarianism, and military despotism, emphasizing the need for an informed citizenry, freedom of speech, and economic welfare. The text examines the historical evolution of democratic institutions, detailing the transition from ancient Greece and Rome through English constitutional struggles from the Magna Carta to universal suffrage, and concludes with the origins of American democracy from colonial self-government to the Declaration of Independence.",
